%   paper has no DOI; full text from the PPIG site,
%   https://www.ppig.org/files/2014-PPIG-25th-Church.pdf.
% PICKED: the only empirical (interview + observation) study of version
%   control users that analyses *why* the conceptual model is hard to hold,
%   in Cognitive Dimensions terms; primary source, cited by later usability
%   work on Git.  Its participants are professionals, which is stated where
%   it is cited: the point is that the hidden state is hard even for them.
% QUOTE (Sec. 5, Hidden Dependencies): "there is a dependency that files on
%   the disk are associated with a branch, another dependency between the
%   local branch and the remote repository and a third that remote branched
%   depend on other branches. [...] New files that have been created, but
%   have not been explicitly 'git add'ed, will be unknown to the version
%   control system."
% QUOTE (Sec. 5): "$ git status / On branch master / Your branch is
%   up-to-date with 'origin/master'. [...] This was actually in a working
%   directory that is hundreds of changes (commits) behind the remote
%   repository, but is up-to-date with respect to local version of the
%   remote repository."
% QUOTE (Sec. 4): "the participants in both studies do understand the VCSs.
%   During the conceptual probing phase, they are able to clearly and
%   precisely articulate the entities and relationships in their version
%   control system. [...] Their conceptual understanding is strong, [...] but
%   this is not sufficient for them to feel confident in its behaviour"
% QUOTE (Sec. 5, conclusion of analysis): "the Hidden Dependencies which
%   leaves the user unclear of the state ('where am I? I'll just reset
%   everything'), [...] ('is it my local or my remote master that my branch
